820
00:30:25,710 --> 00:30:31,590
So first, I wanted to talk a little bit about how GROKBOT came to be.

821
00:30:31,590 --> 00:30:35,910
And I think we're in a pretty interesting moment right now where when you think about

822
00:30:35,910 --> 00:30:41,990
the way that AI has changed and really transformed the way that people and companies do work,

823
00:30:41,990 --> 00:30:44,830
I think we've been through a few different phases.

824
00:30:44,830 --> 00:30:50,870
And I think it started with really chat where you ask a question to AI, AI responds.

825
00:30:50,870 --> 00:30:52,070
And that was really useful.

826
00:30:52,070 --> 00:30:55,870
I think that helps with a bunch of research workflows, for example.

827
00:30:55,870 --> 00:30:59,910
I think the next step after that was you delegate a task to AI.

828
00:30:59,910 --> 00:31:03,590
And there's a copilot that kind of sits next to you while you do your work.

829
00:31:03,590 --> 00:31:08,710
You iterate on a slide deck together, those types of workflows.

830
00:31:08,710 --> 00:31:12,950
But I think we're really on the cusp of this new moment in AI.

831
00:31:12,950 --> 00:31:18,350
Where AI starts to act more like a teammate or more like a colleague inside of a company.

832
00:31:18,350 --> 00:31:20,790
And that's really different, I think, from what's come before.

833
00:31:20,790 --> 00:31:24,990
I think it's going to change the types of products that really meet the moment.

834
00:31:24,990 --> 00:31:29,870
I think the ways that we need to change our ways and how we do work day-to-day to really

835
00:31:29,870 --> 00:31:34,310
maximally get the most out of what AI can do inside of a company.

836
00:31:34,310 --> 00:31:39,990
And then I think what will come after that is kind of this weird world of AI teammates

837
00:31:39,990 --> 00:31:43,550
and human teammates that collaborate together inside of companies.

838
00:31:43,550 --> 00:31:48,230
And where you're not just delegating a discrete task and saying, hey, go from point A to point

839
00:31:48,230 --> 00:31:53,290
be, you're actually doing a lot more kind of what you would do with an actual human

840
00:31:53,290 --> 00:31:57,810
colleague of, they're not a collection of tasks that you kind of outsource, but they own

841
00:31:57,810 --> 00:32:00,130
outcomes and they have responsibilities.

842
00:32:00,130 --> 00:32:06,410
And you trust them to kind of own a discrete part of a company and kind of do that work

843
00:32:06,410 --> 00:32:07,410
for you.

844
00:32:07,410 --> 00:32:10,930
And I think this is really where Grockbot originated from.

845
00:32:10,930 --> 00:32:14,030
And I want to talk a little bit about some of the product decisions that we made to get

846
00:32:14,030 --> 00:32:15,190
there.

847
00:32:15,190 --> 00:32:18,730
So if you haven't used Grockbot, this is what it looks like.

848
00:32:18,730 --> 00:32:23,490
And I think there were a few things that we chose to do with Grockbot that are a bit different

849
00:32:23,490 --> 00:32:28,910
from other AI tools on the market that you maybe have used before are familiar with.

850
00:32:28,910 --> 00:32:34,870
I think the first one is we really wanted Grockbot to feel a lot like you're working with

851
00:32:34,870 --> 00:32:36,170
teammates and colleagues.

852
00:32:36,170 --> 00:32:43,090
And so as a result, it feels kind of simple and chat-like and almost like you're messaging,

853
00:32:43,090 --> 00:32:44,930
your coworkers or teams of colleagues.

854
00:32:44,930 --> 00:32:47,370
So you have on the left all of these bots,

855
00:32:47,370 --> 00:32:50,450
in this case, sales outbound and an inbox manager

856
00:32:50,450 --> 00:32:53,330
that each have specific roles and different kinds

857
00:32:53,330 --> 00:32:54,650
of lanes of work.

858
00:32:54,650 --> 00:32:56,810
And the idea is not that you create a chat

859
00:32:56,810 --> 00:32:58,730
for each task that you want to take on.

860
00:32:58,730 --> 00:33:02,170
You actually create a bot for a unit of work

861
00:33:02,170 --> 00:33:04,290
and a kind of job responsibility.

862
00:33:04,290 --> 00:33:07,170
And you come back to that bot over and over and over again

863
00:33:07,170 --> 00:33:08,370
and it learns from you.

864
00:33:08,370 --> 00:33:10,530
And I think that's one of the most magical moments

865
00:33:10,530 --> 00:33:12,610
of working with Grockbot is you say something

866
00:33:12,610 --> 00:33:16,650
a few weeks ago about, you know, I really like when my slides are formatted in this particular

867
00:33:16,650 --> 00:33:20,810
way. And then Grockbot remembers. It remembers weeks later when you're making your next slide

868
00:33:20,810 --> 00:33:23,810
deck. And that's what you would expect from a colleague. And I think that's what you

869
00:33:23,810 --> 00:33:30,330
should expect from AI as well. So there's this teammate paradigm instead of a task paradigm.

870
00:33:30,330 --> 00:33:35,610
Team mates really sit at the center of Grockbot in the way to work with AI. The second thing

871
00:33:35,610 --> 00:33:41,250
that's a little bit different is Grockbot, similar to what you would expect from a colleague,

872
00:33:41,250 --> 00:33:46,250
doesn't just have access to direct integrations and MCPs and APIs.

873
00:33:46,250 --> 00:33:51,250
Those are very useful, but they oftentimes don't get you 100% of the way to completing the

874
00:33:51,250 --> 00:33:56,410
work that you want. They have access to their own computer, and they can use that computer

875
00:33:56,410 --> 00:34:01,490
exactly how you would. They can watch a video, they can listen to a podcast, they can click

876
00:34:01,490 --> 00:34:08,450
in some, you know, antiquated government software from the 90s. It can do everything that

877
00:34:08,450 --> 00:34:11,570
a human can do with a computer, but it's yours.

878
00:34:11,570 --> 00:34:14,890
And in addition to all of the integrations and MCPs

879
00:34:14,890 --> 00:34:17,850
and things that you come to know and love with AI,

880
00:34:17,850 --> 00:34:22,330
I think it really opens up a new set of tasks and opportunities

881
00:34:22,330 --> 00:34:24,290
for what AI can take off of your play.

882
00:34:24,290 --> 00:34:26,410
And I think it feels really different

883
00:34:26,410 --> 00:34:29,530
when you work with AI that can do those things versus can't.

884
00:34:29,530 --> 00:34:32,370
And the difference between getting kind of 90% of the way

885
00:34:32,370 --> 00:34:34,970
there, like almost having the job be done

886
00:34:34,970 --> 00:34:37,370
versus the thing just being done and to end,

887
00:34:37,370 --> 00:34:40,210
kind of hands off, you trust that your bot can do it,

888
00:34:40,210 --> 00:34:42,610
feels very different, feels really magical.

889
00:34:42,610 --> 00:34:45,770
And then the last thing is everything runs in the cloud.

890
00:34:45,770 --> 00:34:47,610
It doesn't run on your computer.

891
00:34:47,610 --> 00:34:50,170
And I think there's kind of in this weird moment in AI

892
00:34:50,170 --> 00:34:53,250
right now where a lot of the workflows that people

893
00:34:53,250 --> 00:34:57,010
rely so much on AI for, it runs on their local laptop,

894
00:34:57,010 --> 00:34:58,410
which is kind of strange.

895
00:34:58,410 --> 00:35:02,010
And so if you're like asleep and your computer is closed,

896
00:35:02,010 --> 00:35:04,610
the automation that you set up to run it to AM

897
00:35:04,610 --> 00:35:06,210
doesn't fire.

898
00:35:06,210 --> 00:35:08,250
and you wake up in the morning and you're confused,

899
00:35:08,250 --> 00:35:10,410
and you want to kick something off from your phone,

900
00:35:10,410 --> 00:35:13,130
but it's tethered to your laptop, back in your office.

901
00:35:13,130 --> 00:35:14,570
It's just all this weirdness.

902
00:35:14,570 --> 00:35:16,650
And we wanted to just jump right past that

903
00:35:16,650 --> 00:35:20,250
to a future where you have these very smart colleagues,

904
00:35:20,250 --> 00:35:23,370
these teammates, these bots that live in the cloud.

905
00:35:23,370 --> 00:35:25,730
And you can interact with them anywhere you work.

906
00:35:25,730 --> 00:35:27,650
It might be on your phone, it might be on your laptop,

907
00:35:27,650 --> 00:35:30,610
and the future, it might be something entirely different.

908
00:35:30,610 --> 00:35:32,610
And that really informed all of the decisions

909
00:35:32,610 --> 00:35:35,010
we made for the product was those three things.

910
00:35:35,010 --> 00:35:37,590
It feels like interacting with teammates, everything

911
00:35:37,590 --> 00:35:40,930
runs in the cloud, and your bots have access to a computer

912
00:35:40,930 --> 00:35:43,770
that they can drive and manipulate just like you can.

913
00:35:43,770 --> 00:35:48,250
And I think part of the reason we really felt the desire

914
00:35:48,250 --> 00:35:50,690
and the excitement to build something like Rockbot

915
00:35:50,690 --> 00:35:52,690
is from what we've seen in coding.

916
00:35:52,690 --> 00:35:54,530
And I think from the past two years,

917
00:35:54,530 --> 00:35:58,730
we've seen the job of a developer completely transform.

918
00:35:58,730 --> 00:36:02,890
And I think a very stark, hard to overestimate how big the change

919
00:36:02,890 --> 00:36:07,810
has been from an era of writing individual lines of code

920
00:36:07,810 --> 00:36:10,970
by hand to, I think, what a lot of development looks like now.

921
00:36:10,970 --> 00:36:13,930
Certainly inside of SpaceX AI, where you have developers

922
00:36:13,930 --> 00:36:16,570
that own outcomes, they own direction,

923
00:36:16,570 --> 00:36:19,010
and they steer their team of agents

924
00:36:19,010 --> 00:36:22,050
to complete these discrete tasks and do kind of a lot

925
00:36:22,050 --> 00:36:23,970
of the mundane work for them.

926
00:36:23,970 --> 00:36:26,530
And we haven't seen that for knowledge work at all.

927
00:36:26,530 --> 00:36:28,770
I think when you look at the way that knowledge work

928
00:36:28,770 --> 00:36:32,410
is done today versus the way it's done in 2024 or 2025,

929
00:36:32,410 --> 00:36:34,130
It's not that different.

930
00:36:34,130 --> 00:36:37,450
And I think our hope and goal for GROKBA

931
00:36:37,450 --> 00:36:39,770
is to really be the most useful way

932
00:36:39,770 --> 00:36:44,770
to harness these models inside of a company to do real work.

933
00:36:44,770 --> 00:36:46,650
And so I touched on some of these points,

934
00:36:46,650 --> 00:36:50,570
but I'm Rita will talk a little bit more about specific ways

935
00:36:50,570 --> 00:36:52,970
that we're seeing GROKBA be useful for us internally

936
00:36:52,970 --> 00:36:55,850
but also inside of real companies in the wild.

937
00:36:55,850 --> 00:36:58,410
But we wanted it to be delightfully simple.

938
00:36:58,410 --> 00:37:02,090
And we think the future of AI products

939
00:37:02,090 --> 00:37:04,290
really should be very simple to use,

940
00:37:04,290 --> 00:37:06,130
very simple to get started on,

941
00:37:06,130 --> 00:37:10,050
and then gradually pull you into the more complex capabilities.

942
00:37:10,050 --> 00:37:12,330
And I think a lot of people's experience using Grakbot

943
00:37:12,330 --> 00:37:13,610
is you meet it on its face,

944
00:37:13,610 --> 00:37:15,330
you kind of give it an initial task

945
00:37:15,330 --> 00:37:17,850
that is a little straightforward and simple.

946
00:37:17,850 --> 00:37:20,570
Grakbot does it, you give it something more ambitious.

947
00:37:20,570 --> 00:37:23,010
Grakbot does it, you give it something even more ambitious,

948
00:37:23,010 --> 00:37:25,210
and you just kind of keep going through that process

949
00:37:25,210 --> 00:37:28,810
until you feel the limits of maybe where current AI capabilities

950
00:37:28,810 --> 00:37:29,570
are.

951
00:37:29,570 --> 00:37:33,410
And our bat is these models are going to keep getting smarter.

952
00:37:33,410 --> 00:37:37,170
We want to give them a safe environment to run in to really

953
00:37:37,170 --> 00:37:41,010
maximally be useful to employees of companies.

954
00:37:41,010 --> 00:37:42,170
So it's very simple.

955
00:37:42,170 --> 00:37:43,850
It's always on.

956
00:37:43,850 --> 00:37:46,850
It's this teammate in the cloud that you can always depend on

957
00:37:46,850 --> 00:37:50,210
and rely on and never goes down even when you're asleep

958
00:37:50,210 --> 00:37:51,970
or when your computer is closed.

959
00:37:51,970 --> 00:37:53,410
And it really finishes the work.

960
00:37:53,410 --> 00:37:55,450
You give it outcomes, it delivers.

961
00:37:55,450 --> 00:37:58,210
And I think the next step of this will be how this works

962
00:37:58,210 --> 00:37:59,490
inside of teams.

963
00:37:59,490 --> 00:38:03,050
I think right now AI is largely a single player game.

964
00:38:03,050 --> 00:38:04,770
You work with your personal AI system

965
00:38:04,770 --> 00:38:05,810
on various things.

966
00:38:05,810 --> 00:38:09,250
I think we're really excited about what shared AI colleagues

967
00:38:09,250 --> 00:38:12,490
could look like and what the future of internal tooling

