What are the priorities?

6880
05:55:11,770 --> 05:55:14,650
These are questions that now every product builder,

6881
05:55:14,650 --> 05:55:18,450
whether you're formally engineer or designer or founder

6882
05:55:18,450 --> 05:55:21,730
or product manager really has to grapple with

6883
05:55:21,730 --> 05:55:25,530
as you start to build software for your customers,

6884
05:55:25,530 --> 05:55:27,230
for your users.

6885
05:55:27,230 --> 05:55:30,030
Yeah, so with that, we thought we'd spend just a second

6886
05:55:30,030 --> 05:55:32,350
talking about the motivation behind Rockbot

6887
05:55:32,350 --> 05:55:35,670
because in some ways, Rockbot is the product we've also made

6888
05:55:35,670 --> 05:55:38,470
and so there's a story to it having been built

6889
05:55:38,470 --> 05:55:40,390
just the same way there's a story to every product

6890
05:55:40,390 --> 05:55:41,590
having been built.

6891
05:55:41,590 --> 05:55:44,830
And I think if you think back to sort of what motivated

6892
05:55:44,830 --> 05:55:47,130
rock bot in the early early days.

6893
05:55:47,130 --> 05:55:49,510
It was realizing that at least internally

6894
05:55:49,510 --> 05:55:52,110
within SpaceX AI, there are so many folks who

6895
05:55:52,110 --> 05:55:55,010
were starting to evolve beyond a chat box.

6896
05:55:55,010 --> 05:55:56,950
And I think there's been a lot of discourse about this.

6897
05:55:56,950 --> 05:56:00,590
Maybe some of you all have felt this or read about it as well.

6898
05:56:00,590 --> 05:56:03,710
But really it started to feel increasingly that agents

6899
05:56:03,710 --> 05:56:07,550
weren't quite as home in a chat box as we might have wanted

6900
05:56:07,550 --> 05:56:08,050
them to be.

6901
05:56:08,050 --> 05:56:10,470
I think a lot of this was just a natural evolution

6902
05:56:10,470 --> 05:56:12,670
about how we started interacting with these large language

6903
05:56:12,670 --> 05:56:18,750
models and sort of going from asking for a response and maybe waiting for some text to

6904
05:56:18,750 --> 05:56:20,790
asking agents to drive outcomes.

6905
05:56:20,790 --> 05:56:25,670
And so obviously we saw a lot of this outcome-based agentic development happen when it comes

6906
05:56:25,670 --> 05:56:27,070
to things like coding agents.

6907
05:56:27,070 --> 05:56:32,070
But as more and more of us internally started thinking about that beyond coding, it felt

6908
05:56:32,070 --> 05:56:34,110
like the chat box was a little bit limiting.

6909
05:56:34,110 --> 05:56:38,910
And so we started exploring all kinds of ways that we might want to chat and work with

6910
05:56:38,910 --> 05:56:43,070
agents and have them complete tasks and have access to systems that really go

6911
05:56:43,070 --> 05:56:47,390
beyond the chat box. And you know, the most common thing that kept coming up in

6912
05:56:47,390 --> 05:56:52,350
all of these conversations was that we kept sort of treating our agents as if

6913
05:56:52,350 --> 05:56:55,350
they were colleagues. We wanted to be able to interact with them as if they were

6914
05:56:55,350 --> 05:56:59,790
sort of these smart colleagues that sat next to us at our desks or were

6915
05:56:59,790 --> 05:57:04,630
messageable and slack or had access to linear to look at tickets or you know

6916
05:57:04,630 --> 05:57:08,670
wrote PRDs with us in notion or whatever it is. And so a lot of the

6917
05:57:08,670 --> 05:57:14,310
motivation behind how broadband was involved and why it is the way it is today is grounded

6918
05:57:14,310 --> 05:57:19,030
in this idea that we should really have these agentic colleagues that we can work with

6919
05:57:19,030 --> 05:57:21,030
on all the right things we do when we build products.

6920
05:57:21,030 --> 05:57:22,750
And so that was really the motivation.

6921
05:57:22,750 --> 05:57:27,550
So really I think there's four things that we kind of took as a brief based on that

6922
05:57:27,550 --> 05:57:29,830
colleague definition.

6923
05:57:29,830 --> 05:57:35,470
The first one is that colleagues, when you work with them, often tie multiple tools together.

6924
05:57:35,470 --> 05:57:37,070
They don't live in one system.

6925
05:57:37,070 --> 05:57:38,950
They work across many systems.

6926
05:57:38,950 --> 05:57:40,630
And those systems do different things, right?

6927
05:57:40,630 --> 05:57:41,790
So you might have a ticketing system.

6928
05:57:41,790 --> 05:57:44,230
Like I mentioned, linear or ger something like it.

6929
05:57:44,230 --> 05:57:48,430
You might be using CRM or CRM++, something like Salesforce

6930
05:57:48,430 --> 05:57:49,230
or more.

6931
05:57:49,230 --> 05:57:52,910
You might be keeping documents in Notion, designs in Vigma.

6932
05:57:52,910 --> 05:57:55,950
And all these tools are really, really great at what they do.

6933
05:57:55,950 --> 05:57:58,910
But oftentimes, your colleagues or yourselves

6934
05:57:58,910 --> 05:58:02,040
are tying things together across them.

6935
05:58:02,040 --> 05:58:04,120
The other things here that these colleagues

6936
05:58:04,120 --> 05:58:06,840
often have long running contexts.

6937
05:58:06,840 --> 05:58:10,520
They learn and they don't just work on a task,

6938
05:58:10,520 --> 05:58:12,200
eject the task, and then move on.

6939
05:58:12,200 --> 05:58:13,560
The best colleagues you work with,

6940
05:58:13,560 --> 05:58:15,240
the best teammates you work with,

6941
05:58:15,240 --> 05:58:17,200
they learn on the job, right?

6942
05:58:17,200 --> 05:58:20,040
Maybe they start out with very little company context.

6943
05:58:20,040 --> 05:58:21,880
You teach them some tasks or teach them how to get

6944
05:58:21,880 --> 05:58:23,120
some work done.

6945
05:58:23,120 --> 05:58:24,000
They kind of do some work.

6946
05:58:24,000 --> 05:58:25,440
You give them some feedback, right?

6947
05:58:25,440 --> 05:58:26,640
That wasn't that great.

6948
05:58:26,640 --> 05:58:30,200
They hopefully take the feedback and get better at their jobs.

6949
05:58:30,200 --> 05:58:32,200
We think that model generalizes to agents, too,

6950
05:58:32,200 --> 05:58:34,000
where agents can really, you know,

6951
05:58:34,000 --> 05:58:35,240
the way that the models have evolved,

6952
05:58:35,240 --> 05:58:38,720
get good at learning, as sort of building memory for themselves,

6953
05:58:38,720 --> 05:58:40,400
at realizing what's important,

6954
05:58:40,400 --> 05:58:43,810
and at taking and incorporating that kind of feedback.

6955
05:58:43,810 --> 05:58:45,930
The third thing, and I think this is a really big one,

6956
05:58:45,930 --> 05:58:47,050
is independence.

6957
05:58:47,050 --> 05:58:49,570
And I think for a lot of, whoops, you lost the slides.

6958
05:58:49,570 --> 05:58:51,010
Let's go fix that.

6959
05:58:51,010 --> 05:58:52,250
There we go.

6960
05:58:52,250 --> 05:58:54,450
For a lot of people we work with,

6961
05:58:54,450 --> 05:58:56,210
they go execute things independently, right?

6962
05:58:56,210 --> 05:58:58,170
Once in a while, maybe you'll get asked for,

6963
05:58:58,170 --> 05:59:00,210
hey, I need some help on blocking a task,

6964
05:59:00,210 --> 05:59:03,250
or maybe you someone gets stuck on,

6965
05:59:03,250 --> 05:59:06,170
stuck with access to a system or something like this.

6966
05:59:06,170 --> 05:59:08,810
But really, the defining property of a colleague

6967
05:59:08,810 --> 05:59:10,490
that you work with, one of the defining properties

6968
05:59:10,490 --> 05:59:11,930
is that they can work on their own,

6969
05:59:11,930 --> 05:59:13,810
they can complete tasks on their own,

6970
05:59:13,810 --> 05:59:15,210
and they have their own access.

6971
05:59:15,210 --> 05:59:17,570
And so this is really what motivated the need

6972
05:59:17,570 --> 05:59:19,490
for GROKBOT to have its own computer.

6973
05:59:19,490 --> 05:59:21,970
And so we decided GROKBOT should have its own computer

6974
05:59:21,970 --> 05:59:23,530
that I can do things in.

6975
05:59:23,530 --> 05:59:25,450
And this is true, and this is how the product works today.

6976
05:59:25,450 --> 05:59:27,610
So if you go in and use GROKBOT,

6977
05:59:27,610 --> 05:59:29,610
GROKBOT has its own computer, you can kind of help it

6978
05:59:29,610 --> 05:59:31,490
when it gets stuck on that computer,

6979
05:59:31,490 --> 05:59:34,130
but it has its own surface where it can create the output

6980
05:59:34,130 --> 05:59:37,440
that you need to get assisted in your work.

6981
05:59:37,440 --> 05:59:39,340
And then the last and final thing I'll call out here

6982
05:59:39,340 --> 05:59:40,180
is messaging.

6983
05:59:40,180 --> 05:59:43,020
So earlier I mentioned that it felt like we

6984
05:59:43,020 --> 05:59:46,780
hadn't, we wanted to move beyond the chat box a little bit.

6985
05:59:46,780 --> 05:59:49,020
But I meant that a little bit tongue in cheek.

6986
05:59:49,020 --> 05:59:51,340
The chat box that defines how you interact

6987
05:59:51,340 --> 05:59:52,420
with a large language of model.

6988
05:59:52,420 --> 05:59:54,060
We think that core premise may still

6989
05:59:54,060 --> 05:59:55,740
have a lot of sense.

6990
05:59:55,740 --> 05:59:57,900
But where it started to feel a little bit constrained

6991
05:59:57,900 --> 05:59:59,900
that usually when you're chatting with a colleague.

6992
06:00:00,000 --> 06:00:03,120
like rapid fire messaging, if you're like me and Slack or maybe like us and Slack,

6993
06:00:03,120 --> 06:00:06,020
you're constantly streaming out messages, you're switching between threads,

6994
06:00:06,020 --> 06:00:09,000
you're multitasking, and we found that it, you know,

6995
06:00:09,000 --> 06:00:11,880
rather than having to wait your turn for the next response,

6996
06:00:11,880 --> 06:00:15,480
that it's a lot more engaging if you can just really jump in,

6997
06:00:15,480 --> 06:00:18,160
interrupt, drive and interact with agents in this way.

6998
06:00:18,160 --> 06:00:19,440
And you'll see a little bit of this in a minute.

6999
06:00:19,440 --> 06:00:23,120
But really, I think those are the four things that we found as we kind of built

7000
06:00:23,120 --> 06:00:26,080
this product and play with it internally that, you know, define how colleagues work.

7001
06:00:26,560 --> 06:00:27,560
I love those four things.

7002
06:00:27,560 --> 06:00:29,200
And it's not just like any colleague.

7003
06:00:29,200 --> 06:00:31,720
It's like really trying to encode the best colleagues

7004
06:00:31,720 --> 06:00:33,000
that you have worked with.

7005
06:00:33,000 --> 06:00:33,800
Yeah.

7006
06:00:33,800 --> 06:00:37,520
They have a ton of contacts about the organization.

7007
06:00:37,520 --> 06:00:39,960
They understand and can learn on the job.

7008
06:00:39,960 --> 06:00:42,440
They can move independently, make their own decisions.

7009
06:00:42,440 --> 06:00:45,440
They're not constantly waiting for you.

7010
06:00:45,440 --> 06:00:47,480
And so yeah, I think this is like the high level

7011
06:00:47,480 --> 06:00:49,760
of how we think about what Grockbot is.

7012
06:00:49,760 --> 06:00:52,480
It's an agent with a computer at the end of the day.

7013
06:00:52,480 --> 06:00:55,620
And it feels like texting a colleague.

7014
06:00:55,620 --> 06:00:56,120
Exactly.

7015
06:00:56,120 --> 06:00:57,020
Yeah.

7016
06:00:57,020 --> 06:01:01,100
And I think there's two things here that if you want to sort of take away with you as

7017
06:01:01,100 --> 06:01:03,820
to what Rockbot can do is it's these two things on this slide here.

7018
06:01:03,820 --> 06:01:07,660
It's that we want these Rockbot to be AI teammates that you can give real work to and

7019
06:01:07,660 --> 06:01:09,100
that come back with results.

7020
06:01:09,100 --> 06:01:11,860
They don't come back with text or they don't come back with sort of questions that come

7021
06:01:11,860 --> 06:01:12,860
back with results.

7022
06:01:12,860 --> 06:01:15,620
And then I think the last thing here is that they really finish jobs.

7023
06:01:15,620 --> 06:01:20,260
We think that really being able to get the job done is a much more productive way to interact

7024
06:01:20,260 --> 06:01:22,460
with agents and come back when they need approval.

7025
06:01:22,460 --> 06:01:23,460
So yeah, I'm curious.

7026
06:01:23,460 --> 06:01:24,780
How do we use these with products?

7027
06:01:24,780 --> 06:01:35,780
So we are actually going to see Grocbot in the wild, a real demo of some of the agents that we have built the colleagues that we work with in Grocbot.

7028
06:01:35,780 --> 06:01:39,780
I think there are really sort of three use cases that we're going to see here.

7029
06:01:39,780 --> 06:01:51,780
The first is what we call the attention list. And so as PMs, we're constantly pulled in a bunch of different directions. Maybe you're working across a bunch of different projects or there's a bunch of context everywhere.

7030
06:01:51,780 --> 06:02:10,780
very helpful to think about what are you currently paying attention to? What have you been paying attention to this morning, this week, this month, and then really dip that against your priority list and help you understand how the ways in which you're spending your time and where your focus is relative to what needs to get done across the team.

7031
06:02:10,780 --> 06:02:16,260
And we're going to talk about how Brockbot can help you do that as this helpful assistant,

7032
06:02:16,260 --> 06:02:21,220
this colleague that's always on your shoulder, being able to learn about the ways in which

7033
06:02:21,220 --> 06:02:22,420
you're working.

7034
06:02:22,420 --> 06:02:26,060
The next is research and building up customer context.

7035
06:02:26,060 --> 06:02:30,760
Obviously, this is where a lot of product work goes into doing the research, figuring out

7036
06:02:30,760 --> 06:02:36,860
what users and customers actually need and want and love, what the biggest bottlenecks

7037
06:02:36,860 --> 06:02:40,060
are, what issues are they running into?

7038
06:02:40,060 --> 06:02:41,860
How can you better serve them?

7039
06:02:41,860 --> 06:02:44,260
And so being able to gather up all that context,

7040
06:02:44,260 --> 06:02:48,760
learn from your users, be able to synthesize data in new ways.

7041
06:02:48,760 --> 06:02:50,860
GROCKBOT is actually really phenomenal for

7042
06:02:50,860 --> 06:02:52,860
and we're going to talk about some of the data science

7043
06:02:52,860 --> 06:02:56,500
and analytics and product use cases

7044
06:02:56,500 --> 06:02:58,500
that we turned to GROCKBOT for.

7045
06:02:58,500 --> 06:02:59,980
And then of course shipping,

7046
06:02:59,980 --> 06:03:02,940
you cannot be a product manager in 2026

7047
06:03:02,940 --> 06:03:07,300
if you are not focused on delivering software products

7048
06:03:07,300 --> 06:03:08,700
to your customers.

7049
06:03:08,700 --> 06:03:14,540
And so really thinking about how GROKBOT can not only accelerate you as a PM, but also

7050
06:03:14,540 --> 06:03:15,540
the entire team.

7051
06:03:15,540 --> 06:03:16,540
Yeah.

7052
06:03:16,540 --> 06:03:20,620
There's a stat on the slide here. GROKBOT represents for us internally a double digit percentage

7053
06:03:20,620 --> 06:03:26,380
of merged PRs. And I think that's a pretty wild stat. It also within the product organization,

7054
06:03:26,380 --> 06:03:30,420
empowers all of us as product people to be able to ship PRs into production as well.

7055
06:03:30,420 --> 06:03:33,700
And so you can just really get a bunch of superpowers when you can set up this in the

7056
06:03:33,700 --> 06:03:34,700
right way.

7057
06:03:34,700 --> 06:03:35,700
Very cool.

7058
06:03:35,700 --> 06:03:39,300
So let us with that meet the Grockbot team

7059
06:03:39,300 --> 06:03:41,620
that we will be working with today.

7060
06:03:41,620 --> 06:03:45,620
Some familiar faces and names that we can all learn.

7061
06:03:45,620 --> 06:03:48,900
So first is Korra, Chief of Staff.

7062
06:03:48,900 --> 06:03:51,060
You can think of this as sort of the person

7063
06:03:51,060 --> 06:03:54,300
that you turn to have access to your email,

7064
06:03:54,300 --> 06:03:56,420
has access to your calendar.

7065
06:03:56,420 --> 06:03:59,180
Maybe listen on Slack for some of the messages

7066
06:03:59,180 --> 06:04:02,940
that you're sending and really builds up a model

7067
06:04:02,940 --> 06:04:08,530
of how you as APM actually work.

7068
06:04:08,530 --> 06:04:10,290
Next is Emily.

7069
06:04:10,290 --> 06:04:12,970
Emily is an engineering manager.

7070
06:04:13,970 --> 06:04:18,210
Emily has been coached not to actually do the coding,

7071
06:04:18,210 --> 06:04:21,370
but just to manage a team of engineers

7072
06:04:21,370 --> 06:04:23,090
that we have given Emily.

7073
06:04:23,090 --> 06:04:27,170
And so Emily has five different engineering colleagues

7074
06:04:27,170 --> 06:04:30,290
in Grocbot that she manages.

7075
06:04:30,290 --> 06:04:34,130
It's very helpful to have Emily as sort of this one contact

7076
06:04:34,130 --> 06:04:38,510
across the engineering team to be able to give larger chunks

7077
06:04:38,510 --> 06:04:42,270
of work, have Emily then deconstruct that and delegate

7078
06:04:42,270 --> 06:04:45,050
that work, actually run the verification loops

7079
06:04:45,050 --> 06:04:48,890
for the outputs of some of the engineering agents.

7080
06:04:48,890 --> 06:04:52,010
We'll have Ashley the go to for data science,

7081
06:04:52,010 --> 06:04:56,170
for analytics, for being able to ask and answer data questions.

7082
06:04:56,170 --> 06:04:58,810
I think as a PM oftentimes throughout the day,

7083
06:04:58,810 --> 06:05:02,090
there is like a random conversation that sparks an idea

7084
06:05:02,090 --> 06:05:06,490
that would have previously required you to go write a bunch of SQL and figure out where

7085
06:05:06,490 --> 06:05:11,290
the trusted data sources are, and very helpful to have somebody that you can just quickly

7086
06:05:11,290 --> 06:05:18,410
message and get an answer, a chart, a synthesis of the underlying data set that will help

7087
06:05:18,410 --> 06:05:21,630
inform some of your decisions throughout the day.

7088
06:05:21,630 --> 06:05:22,790
We also have PMP.

7089
06:05:22,790 --> 06:05:30,790
PMP is a trusted sidekick for the product team, being able to draft up some product requirement

7090
06:05:30,790 --> 06:05:34,950
docs synthesize some of the customer and user insights

7091
06:05:34,950 --> 06:05:39,590
and be able to run some of the lower level product work

7092
06:05:39,590 --> 06:05:40,940
day to day.

7093
06:05:40,940 --> 06:05:42,060
You want to talk about Pixel?

7094
06:05:42,060 --> 06:05:43,340
Yeah, Pixel's one of my favorites.

7095
06:05:43,340 --> 06:05:45,940
Pixel is a designer.

7096
06:05:45,940 --> 06:05:49,300
And so it has been trained on some of the best advice

7097
06:05:49,300 --> 06:05:52,420
for how to be a world class AI designer.

7098
06:05:52,420 --> 06:05:55,220
It turns out that there are ways to make AI design look

7099
06:05:55,220 --> 06:05:57,980
really good if you instruct your agents and bots

7100
06:05:57,980 --> 06:05:58,500
in the right way.

7101
06:05:58,500 --> 06:06:04,900
So pixels been loaded with a ton of context and how to be an S tier designer from some of the best and latest thinking on the internet.

7102
06:06:05,960 --> 06:06:15,200
Amazing. And finally we have Ray who is a recruiter. So anybody who's in product leadership or building out the product function or building out your company.

7103
06:06:16,000 --> 06:06:21,920
Very helpful to have a colleague that you can turn to on the people and recruiting side of the house.

7104
06:06:22,520 --> 06:06:26,320
To help with some of the sourcing and managing some of the hiring pipeline.

7105
06:06:26,320 --> 06:06:29,800
And so these are some of the very helpful

7106
06:06:29,800 --> 06:06:33,120
Groc bot colleagues that we have defined,

7107
06:06:33,120 --> 06:06:37,800
but it is totally up to you exactly what your team is going to look like.

7108
06:06:37,800 --> 06:06:39,600
And so throughout the course of this demo,

7109
06:06:39,600 --> 06:06:42,560
use this as inspiration, use this for ideas.

7110
06:06:42,560 --> 06:06:45,480
But at the end of the day, you can construct your team,

7111
06:06:45,480 --> 06:06:47,040
figure out who is most helpful,

7112
06:06:47,040 --> 06:06:49,560
what are the sets of roles that will be most

7113
06:06:49,560 --> 06:06:52,080
accelerative for you and your product team.

7114
06:06:52,080 --> 06:06:53,000
Yeah, definitely.

7115
06:06:53,000 --> 06:06:54,720
And I think to that point, you know,

7116
06:06:54,720 --> 06:06:58,000
what we've seen a lot of folks do is they create many agents and so I'm

